     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Matthieu,<div><br></div><div>Colorate helps generate nice palettes given a specific color. Here&#39;s an example with the yellow you provided:</div><div><a href=\"http://colorate.azurewebsites.net/Color/DAAA00\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://colorate.azurewebsites.net/Color/DAAA00&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792465850653000&amp;usg=AOvVaw141xRxFvW6AUKGOrXWBf2a\">http://colorate.azurewebsites.net/Color/DAAA00</a></div><div><br></div><div>Chroma.js is also great for generating balanced, multi-hued sequential/diverging palettes (<a href=\"https://www.vis4.net/blog/2013/09/mastering-multi-hued-color-scales/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.vis4.net/blog/2013/09/mastering-multi-hued-color-scales/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792465850653000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0X5IM9DhYzgik73hlQofas\">https://www.vis4.net/blog/2013/09/mastering-multi-hued-color-scales/</a>). The nice thing about it is that you can specify the number and types of colors you want. So you can combine colorate&#39;s suggestions with chroma&#39;s palette generation, using UC Davis&#39; colors as a reference. </div><div><br></div><div>e.g. <a href=\"https://gka.github.io/palettes/#colors=%23002855,%23DAAA00%7Csteps=7%7Cbez=1%7CcoL=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://gka.github.io/palettes/%23colors%3D%2523002855,%2523DAAA00%257Csteps%3D7%257Cbez%3D1%257CcoL%3D1&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792465850653000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1yU08arZrk1PDzCw978hes\">https://gka.github.io/palettes/#colors=#002855,#DAAA00|steps=7|bez=1|coL=1</a></div><div><br></div><div>Hope that&#39;s helpful!</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Nistara</div><div><br></div><div>On Wednesday, May 15, 2019 at 1:44:22 PM UTC-7, Matthieu Stigler wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi</div><div><br></div><div>I am wondering whether anyone here played once trying to get a nice UC Davis-like color palette for plotting graphs? I found UC Davis colors definition <a href=\"https://marketingtoolbox.ucdavis.edu/visual-identity/color.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://marketingtoolbox.ucdavis.edu/visual-identity/color.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792465850653000&amp;usg=AOvVaw12RU7TuoXkrjv7_pddVOLi\">here</a>, they give also lighter shades of their blue and yellow. I realize any attempt to define a custom palette raises issues that the result might not be state of the art (perception-neutral, color-blind-safe etc) but I am ready to compromise (some) science for aesthetics. <br></div><div><br></div><div>I am mainly looking for a qualitative one (for sequential, could just use their lighter shades?). Thinking of mixing 1-2 blues and 1-2 yellows, though result could be heavily &quot;perception non-neutral&quot; ? See below my first attempt.<br></div><div><br></div><div>Another approach could be to simply look for existing palettes that resemble the UC Davis colors. On the sequential side, I feel the viridis one is a close match!?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks!</div><div><br></div><div>Matthieu</div><div><br></div><div><div><pre style=\"font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;white-space:pre-wrap;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);display:block;padding:0.5em;color:rgb(51,51,51);background:rgb(248,248,248) none repeat scroll 0% 0%\"><span style=\"color:rgb(51,51,51);font-weight:bold\">library</span>(ggplot2)\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(153,153,136);font-style:italic\">#&gt; Registered S3 methods overwritten by &#39;ggplot2&#39;:</span>\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(153,153,136);font-style:italic\">#&gt;   method         from </span>\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(153,153,136);font-style:italic\">#&gt;   [.quosures     rlang</span>\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(153,153,136);font-style:italic\">#&gt;   c.quosures     rlang</span>\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(153,153,136);font-style:italic\">#&gt;   print.quosures rlang</span>\r\n\r\ncols &lt;- c(<span style=\"color:rgb(221,17,68)\">&quot;#DAAA00&quot;</span>, <span style=\"color:rgb(221,17,68)\">&quot;#002855&quot;</span>, <span style=\"color:rgb(221,17,68)\">&quot;#E9CC66&quot;</span>, <span style=\"color:rgb(221,17,68)\">&quot;#667D9D&quot;</span>)\r\nseq_cols &lt;- seq_len(length(cols))\r\n\r\nqplot(x=seq_cols, y= seq_cols, geom=<span style=\"color:rgb(221,17,68)\">&quot;col&quot;</span>, fill= factor(seq_cols)) +\r\n  scale_fill_manual(values = cols)\r\n</code></pre>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em\"><img src=\"https://ci4.googleusercontent.com/proxy/zUMYkLVceL5-L54HYb_bOoi6MjD094w3yStu7clw7WXhW2TnfmzVn5DrB6OK5Piacmo=s0-d-e1-ft#https://i.imgur.com/nxx8cHS.png\" alt=\"\"></p>\r\n<div title=\"MDH:YGBgIHI8YnI+bGlicmFyeShnZ3Bsb3QyKTxicj4jJmd0OyBSZWdpc3RlcmVkIFMzIG1ldGhvZHMg\r\nb3ZlcndyaXR0ZW4gYnkgJ2dncGxvdDInOjxicj4jJmd0O8KgwqAgbWV0aG9kwqDCoMKgwqDCoMKg\r\nwqDCoCBmcm9tIDxicj4jJmd0O8KgwqAgWy5xdW9zdXJlc8KgwqDCoMKgIHJsYW5nPGJyPiMmZ3Q7\r\nwqDCoCBjLnF1b3N1cmVzwqDCoMKgwqAgcmxhbmc8YnI+IyZndDvCoMKgIHByaW50LnF1b3N1cmVz\r\nIHJsYW5nPGJyPjxicj5jb2xzICZsdDstIGMoIiNEQUFBMDAiLCAiIzAwMjg1NSIsICIjRTlDQzY2\r\nIiwgIiM2NjdEOUQiKTxicj5zZXFfY29scyAmbHQ7LSBzZXFfbGVuKGxlbmd0aChjb2xzKSk8YnI+\r\nPGJyPnFwbG90KHg9c2VxX2NvbHMsIHk9IHNlcV9jb2xzLCBnZW9tPSJjb2wiLCBmaWxsPSBmYWN0\r\nb3Ioc2VxX2NvbHMpKSArPGJyPsKgIHNjYWxlX2ZpbGxfbWFudWFsKHZhbHVlcyA9IGNvbHMpPGJy\r\nPmBgYDxicj48YnI+IVtdKGh0dHB